aquabank_ATM: 
	vulnerability in withdrawal memo (256B in 64B buffer). 

aquabank_safe:
	diagnostics() function prints the address of printf and itself (can be used as a leak to get libc base address and PIE base address). 
	opensafe() function has a buffer overflow, but only reads 24B into 8B buffer. It cannot be used to write the whole ROP chain because that would be longer than 24B. 
	deposit() function allows us to write on a global buffer. We can use it to write the binsh string and then the ROP chain.
	Before the RIP offset we put the global buffer address, at the RIP offset we put the leave; ret gadget. 
	What eave does is that rsp points to the buffer address, and pops the first value into rpb (not useful, we don't care), then execution continues from the global buffer.
	NX is not a problem because we didn't execute the global buffer, it didn't contain shellcode bytes. It just contained addresses and we jumped to those addresses.	

